faq:
  - q: "How do you write a resume with no work experience?"
    a: "Lead with education, then add projects, internships, volunteer work, relevant coursework, and any freelance or self-directed work. The skills section is critical for entry-level candidates — list every relevant tool, technology, and methodology. Use the summary to frame your positioning: 'Recent computer science graduate with internship experience building production-grade backend systems.' Every line should support the same story."
  - q: "What should an entry-level resume include?"
    a: "Education first (GPA if above 3.3, relevant coursework, honors), Projects (GitHub links, what you built, what technology, what you learned), Internships (even short ones — quantify impact where possible), Skills (all relevant technical skills listed explicitly), Certifications or coursework (AWS, Google Analytics, etc.), Summary (brief — 2–3 sentences establishing identity and readiness)."
  - q: "How long should an entry-level resume be?"
    a: "One page. No exceptions at the entry level. Two pages signals misjudgment of what matters, not more experience."
  - q: "Should an entry-level resume include a summary?"
    a: "Yes — but keep it to 2–3 sentences. State your field, your strongest technical credential, and your readiness to contribute. Example: 'Recent data science graduate with hands-on experience building ML models in Python (scikit-learn, PyTorch). Completed internship at [Company] where I built a recommendation system that improved click-through rate 18%. Targeting data scientist or ML engineer roles.'"
  - q: "What is the most important section of an entry-level resume?"
    a: "The Skills section and the Projects section. These are where most entry-level candidates are weakest. A strong skills section with 15–20 explicit keywords gets you through ATS. Strong projects with quantified outcomes show you can produce, not just study."
